A||||24||Carrolles||Engine seems to be cutting out at low load||||9||
Z||000000||Carrolles||02-03-2000||12:22 AM||||On my way home from work tonight in my newly rebuilt 2.8 (250 miles 85 SE 4 spd) I noticed a shaking like when you let out the clutch with too little throttle.  It happened while driving around 45 to 55 mph.  Giving it a little gas made it stop.<P>It actually felt like the engine was cutting out.  When I'd accelerate it would clear up.  In fact I had it up to about 70 in a 45 mph zone on a very hilly road when I got pulled over by a policeman.<P>I told him that I realized that I was speeding but it was because I just installed a new engine and I thought the fuel pump was cutting out on me and I wanted to get up enough speed to cross the next steep hill.  The fuel pump actually has been whining since the engine swap.  He sympathesized with me and let me go.  Whew!!!<P>Anyway I'm not sure what was actually happening.  I can't drive it to work like this so I won't get a chance to look at it until the weekend.<P>It felt like the engine was bucking.  I have a new urethane dog bone bushing and I checked all the engine/transmission mounts when I swapped engines.  It has run fine until this.<P>When I left the policeman I drove the 45 mph speed limit without incident.  In the driveway it runs fine.<P>When I installed the new longblock I also rebuilt the distributor (new pick-up coil, rotor, cap, plugs, wires, cleaned up everything).  It was sudden and distinct as if the ignition was cutting out.  Could it be an ignition module going out?<P>Your thoughts on this would be greatly appreciated.<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll||||reg||9
Z||000001||Oreif||02-03-2000||02:29 AM||||Just some idea's to check out. It first sounds like you may have a vacuum leak. Did the check engine light come on? As for a bad sensor I would look at the IAC and the TPS. The IAC has a mechanical adjustment as well as it's electrical adjustment. Did you happen to notice if it did it while engine was warming up or after it was running for a while? That's all I could think of off hand for now.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000002||Alex4mula||02-03-2000||09:07 AM||||Something like that happened to me. It was a contact from the tach filter (connector broken exposing contact) touching metal part of the engine and shorting out while accelerating (engine rocking). Other times had been a bad plug wire, but in this case only was noticed under load. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||02-03-2000||01:00 PM||||Thanks for the replies.<P>I had been driving for about 10 minutes at highway speed on hilly terrain.  The engine was at normal temp (195).  There were no check engine lights.<P>I'll look at the tach filter first since it is easy.  The funny thing is that it happened while driving at steady speed.  Accelerating seemed to make the problem go away, hence the visit by the police.<P>I first thought about a loose wire ore something causing the ignition to cut out.  I found the trunk light switch wires hanging loose and thought that might have caused a short to ground but that only turns the light on.  I taped off the wires anyway (switch is broken).<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||Luperman||02-03-2000||05:18 PM||||Hey Carrole,<BR>    I have the same thing going on in my 86 SE v-6 automatic steady speed betwen 50 and 70 the engine bucks, if you let off or speed up it goes away ???? checked timing after the distributorwas replaced made sure the ecm gm made , replaced fuel filter ,pretty sure it is the tps because when I unplugged it seem to run I little better after I plugged it back but after I drove it about ten miles it came back ,and yes after it warms up that is when it happens.||||reg||10
Z||000005||CRAGAR||02-03-2000||08:38 PM||||Carolles, I've got the same exact symptoms that you have.  I have a 3.1 conversion with new everything!  I recently found a split EGR tube and replaced it along with the IAC.  I've checked for shorts and leaks in the ignition and spark plug wiring, and vacuum tested all of the vacuum tubing on the engine.  Someone suggested that I check the half shafts and CV joints.  I haven't had a chance to do that yet.  At WOT everything feels great; but at partial throttle openings the engine seems to be bucking back an forth and I'm afraid it's going to break something.  I hope someone can give us a good lead!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||02-03-2000||10:37 PM||||Well I took it out for a drive after work today to see if I could get it to act up again.  I drove it for about 4 miles and got it up to temperature and everything was working great.  It wouldn't act up at steady speed or accelerating.  However I did not push the car very hard since I didn't want to chance seeing the same cop again.<P>I turned around and got it up to around 45 but left it in 3rd gear to keep the revs up and sure enough it started bucking.  Just a little at first.  After about 15 seconds if intermittent bucking it really started to cut out and it finally set a code.  It is idling very unevenly now also.  While driving when it starts really bucking I put in the clutch to coast and the idle hunts from around 800 to 2500 rpms.<P>I drove home and checked the codes and got a 15 (coolant temperature sensor) and a 35 (idle air control valve).<P>I had installed a new idle air control valve when I put the new engine in but re-used all the other sensors.<P>I looked up the troubleshooting section for both codes in my Helms manual.  This weekend I'll go through the procedure for both sensors.  It suggests that the coolant temp sensor could make the idle air control valve act up if it is going out.<P>I'll let you know what I find.  Hopefully I'll be able to report that all is better.<P>Take care and thanks for the replys.<P>Carroll ||||reg||1
Z||000007||mwbackus||02-03-2000||11:42 PM||||Most likely cause of the cruise speed bucking is an EGR system malfunction. If the EGR selenoid is defective a trouble code will usually set to indicate this, but problems with the rest of the EGR system are more difficult to isolate.  Check for inproper EGR valve operation, leaks in the EGR tube, and associated vacuum lines. Improper ignition timing or fuel injector problems can also cause these symtoms.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||02-04-2000||11:39 AM||||Checked out my 88 Pontiac Sevice Manual and thought I had it until you posted the codes. I'll go ahead and mention it anyway...<P>The troubleshooting for Surge and "Chuggle" (yes, that's what it says) seems to be the closest to to your problem. Described as varying engine power at cruise with no change in throttle. Besides the usual fuel filter, EGR, vacuum leaks, timing, etc... there was one possible cause I found interesting. <P>It says this can be caused by an O2 sensor contaminated with silicon from RTV sealant (particularly interesting for just rebuilt engines). The O2 sensor gives a reading richer than it should be (but not out of range so no code is set). This makes the ECM run the engine too lean. It says the O2 sensor MAY have a white powdery residue.<P>Something to look into if the more obvious problems don't pan out...<P>David.||||reg||3
Z||000009||zman||02-05-2000||12:48 PM||||i had the same thing happen to me just recently. i just replaced the plug wires with taylor 8mm spiro wound wires on my 89 z24 2.8l, dis ignition. i was a lil baffled, but figured i would change the wires back to a oem 7.5 mm res. type (new). Geuss what?, problem went away. i was kinda suprised. I guess the coil packs did'nt like those taylor wires. Hope this helps ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||02-05-2000||09:38 PM||||I talked to Ed Parks and described my problem and he said that the coolant temp sensor could cause the problem that I am having.  He also said don't bother with the Idle Air Control Valve because the coolant temp sensor could cause it to act up.<P>I put in the new Coolant Temp Sensor (AutoZone $7.99) and everything seems to be working fine now.  I disconnected the battery when I did the work which cleared the codes.  I drove it around for about a half hour without any trouble and no new codes.<P>I'm keeping me fingers crossed that this is it.<P>I still haven't solved the transmission axel seal problem but at least it is driveable now.<P>Thanks to all for the responses and I hope this info is of use.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||7
Z||000011||Carrolles||02-07-2000||07:33 AM||||I drove it to work this morning and everything is still OK.||||reg||7
Z||000012||Luperman||02-08-2000||10:25 PM||||Help carrolles, I took your advice and bought a coolant temp sensor (not fan switch) but on inspection I found (3) sensors installed in the the engine 2 are by the thermastat housing 1 house (1) wire and the other has (2) wires and the thrid sensor is mounted in the block by the coil it looks almost like the one that the part store sold me which has two wires and a new connector  terminal. please can you help me I'm at a loss grrrrrrr...||||reg||5
Z||000013||DRH||02-09-2000||01:52 PM||||V6 Temp sensors/switches are as follows:<P>Near t-stat, 1 wire: Cooling fan switch<BR>Near t-stat, 2 wire: ECM coolant temp sensor<BR>Near coil in head, 2 wire: Temp gauge and light.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Luperman||02-10-2000||02:25 AM||||Hey carrolles when I looked at my car tonight before I went into work and noticed the sensor you said was the coolant temp sensorthat one was horizontal.  now the one I thought was the cts is vertical red wire and tan wire (what sensor is this one ) ,it is next to the one wire  horizontal sensor. TIA ||||reg||5
Z||000015||DRH||02-10-2000||01:56 PM||||Luperman, I'm not Carrolles but I am the one that replied to your last post.<P>I believe the ECM temp sensor wires are black and yellow. There is one more temp switch in that area that I had forgotten about. It is for the cold start injector. That MIGHT be the one with red and tan wires. I am at work and don't have my book or car here. Maybe someone else can confirm the EXACT location of the ECM temp sensor??? <BR>Otherwise, I will look tonight and post tommorrow.<P>Sorry if I created any confusion...<BR>David.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Luperman||02-10-2000||11:41 PM||||you know I looked today and sure enough it was right where carrolles explain and it has yellow and black wires mounted horizontaly below the thermostat housing, now this weekend I'll be installing the new sensor and the new connector that came with it they said it seals better than the factory original something like a triple seal on it , thanks for your help will keep you guys posted to see if this fixes things.||||reg||10
Z||000017||Carrolles||02-11-2000||04:21 PM||||Well everything was working fine for the first two trips and then the car started acting up again.   I finally got frustrated and took it to Pontiac ($358.00 and a new ECM)  I'll pick it up tonight and hope for the best.<P>It took them a lot of diagnostic work to get it figured out but the ECM did check out bad.  The mechanic said that would cause check engine lights indicating bad sonsors.<P>So if your car starts bucking violently while cruising and the sensor swap doesn't cure the problem look at your ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000018||mwbackus||02-11-2000||05:01 PM||||I believe you will find out that this is the typical "we dont know what it is so just replace the ECM" effort that dealerships are famous for.  Perhaps then you could consider some of the advise that has been offered in this thread?  We may not all be "Ed Parks", but many of us have solved these problems before!<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000019||Luperman||02-18-2000||02:51 AM||||Hey carrolles so how is your car  doing after the dealer installed the new ecm mine is still bucking how about yours?||||reg||5
Z||000020||WisconsinGT||02-18-2000||11:02 AM||||Well! Now I have a similar problem. At low speeds or when I am dirving the car after a cold start, she bucks. When she warms-up, it performs better, but still not perfect. If anyone has a fix, please let us know. I did put on a new coil, and this is when it started. So I then disconnected the Tach filter, it runs better but not perfect. Don't know what to do next. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Carrolles||02-18-2000||12:46 PM||||I've driven about 200 miles of combined city/highway/mountain backroads during the last week and it is running great.<P>I guess the new ECM did the trick.||||reg||7
Z||000022||Luperman||02-18-2000||09:24 PM||||just got off the phone with the parts place and they said they could test the ecm out of the car to see if it bad ,don't know how accruate since the car is still running but willing to try anything at this point ,seems like it is just been hit and miss here with replacement parts, btu what carrolles said about the ecm being the culprit makes a little since when all this mess began I had the distributor replaced and ecm as I drove it away from the shop heading 600 miles toward home it started acting up so I made a choice to keep going because I had a plane to catch and no time to waste , so maybe just maybe they gave me a bum ecm, can ecm's be crappy right out of the package?||||reg||14
Z||000023||WisconsinGT||02-19-2000||01:57 PM||||UPDATE!!! I changed the plug wires and all is well. (For the moment that is.) I bought Spitfire (SP) wires. I had to sacrifice color for reliability. (They come in Blue.) My car has never run better. Much more power. Makes me wonder if I was running on 5 or less cylinders. Oh well. Also, how are you folks running your wires on a V6? Are you going under/over the Plenum with the wires to go to the front of the engine? My old ones where running under, but this time I ran my wires over. I noticed that they are close to the throttle body. Should be O.K. Yes?||||reg||1
Z||000024||theogre||02-19-2000||08:24 PM||||As long as the wires don't rub on anything or contact anything really hot you can route them however you want.||||reg||1
